# Security rules

## Content Security Policy
- After the shop is removed, remove Stripe/PayPal sources (`js.stripe.com`, `paypal.com`, ...) only if they were needed solely by the shop.
- Never widen a CSP. Never add `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`.
- Add only what is strictly required (for example the form endpoint) and say so in your reply.

## Meta tags versus real HTTP headers
Check how the security headers are set. If they are `<meta http-equiv>` tags,
report to the user that `X-Frame-Options`, `frame-ancestors` and
`X-Content-Type-Options` have no effect there, and that real headers must be set
on the server/host. Do not claim they protect the site.

## Dynamic content
- No `eval()`. No `innerHTML` (or equivalent) with dynamic data.
- The contact form prefill (subject, artwork name) must use `value` / `textContent`. If the artwork name can come from a URL parameter, treat it as untrusted.

## Contact form and honeypot
- Add a hidden honeypot field: `tabindex="-1"`, `autocomplete="off"`, `aria-hidden="true"`, hidden with the `hidden` attribute or an inline style (no new CSS rule).
- If Formspree is used, check in its documentation which field name it recognizes as honeypot (commonly `_gotcha`) and use that name.
- The honeypot reduces spam; it is not a security guarantee.
- The Formspree form ID is public by design, but must stay a placeholder until the owner provides it.

## Secrets and external scripts
- Report any key, token or credential hardcoded in the code.
- For external scripts and styles (Leaflet, etc.): pinned version, `integrity` (SRI) and `crossorigin`. Do not invent hashes: compute them from the official file, or report what is missing.
- Never add a dependency or CDN without approval.
